\multicolumn{2}{r}{98} \\
\cline{3-4}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & 20 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & 14 \\
\cline{4-5}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & 60 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & 56 \\
\cline{5-6}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & & 40 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & & 28 \\
\cline{6-7}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & & & 120 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & & & 112 \\
\cline{7-8}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & & & & 80 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & & & & 70 \\
\cline{8-9}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & & & & & 100 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & & & & & 98 \\
\cline{9-10}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & & & & & & 20 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & & & & & & 14 \\
\cline{10-11}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & & & & & & & 60 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & & & & & & & 56 \\
\cline{11-12}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & & & & & & & & 40 \\
\end{array}
$$
We see that the decimal expansion of $\frac{5}{14}$ repeats after 6 digits: $0.3571428571428\ldots$
Since $1314$ is divisible by $6$, the $1314$th digit past the decimal point is the same as the $6$th digit past the decimal point, which is $\boxed{2}$.
The answer is: 2

